# Swift & macOS Best Practices for Olive

## 1. SwiftUI & State Management
- Use `@Observable` (Observation framework) for modern view models in macOS 14+.
- Avoid memory leaks: ensure tasks and timer publishers cancel appropriately when views disappear (`.onDisappear` or `.task(id:)`).
- Ensure UI updates always dispatch to the `@MainActor`.
- Keep view bodies declarative and lightweight; factor complex sub-views into distinct components.

## 2. macOS System Integration & Concurrency
- Never block the main thread with disk I/O, file scanning, or process execution. Always use Swift Structured Concurrency (`async/await`, `Task.detached`, `TaskGroup`).
- For file deletion, **always** call `FileManager.default.trashItem(at:resultingItemURL:)` to route files through the native macOS Trash.
- When calling system utilities or shell scripts, use `Process` with proper stdout/stderr pipe drains to avoid deadlocks on large buffer outputs.

## 3. Performance & Memory Budget
- Background menu bar monitoring must stay below **30 MB RAM** and **< 0.5% CPU**.
- Throttle high-frequency UI updates (e.g. per-second sensor polling) using `debounce` or explicit timer intervals.
- Use `monospacedDigit()` for all numeric metric labels to prevent layout jitter.

## 4. Design & Human Interface Guidelines
- Use native macOS vibrancy via `NSVisualEffectView` materials (`.sidebar`, `.hudWindow`, `.underWindowBackground`).
- Implement native keyboard shortcuts (e.g. `⌘R` for refresh, `Space` for QuickLook preview, `⌘⌫` for trash).
- Support dynamic dark mode and light mode color adaptations automatically.
